Waits for the compositor when its socket is full, rather than panicking.

xwayland-satellite flushes its requests to the compositor straight after
a client destroys a shm pool, and straight after asking for a selection's
data, unwrapping the result. When the compositor falls behind, as under a
storm of X11 windows on a loaded machine, the socket is full, the flush
fails with WouldBlock, and satellite panics, taking every X11 window with
it. The main loop's own flush waits up to 50 ms for room, but its retry
after that wait unwraps too. All three now share one flush. The main
loop's waits 50 ms in all, then logs and leaves the rest queued. A shm
pool's Destroy leaves a full socket to that flush, which ends the same
turn, so a burst of destroys waits once rather than once each. A
selection's waits as long as it takes, since its data is read at once
and the selection's owner writes it only once the compositor has the
request to pass on. What is left queued goes out at a later flush, and
the main loop, which polled the compositor's socket only for reading,
now also waits for room to write while anything is left, since nothing
else wakes it once the compositor has read what it was sent. Leaving it
queued is safe only from wayland-backend 0.3.12, which buffers without
bound, so newer-backend.patch takes 0.3.17, as upstream has.

